# Source of truth for page copy. Facts are limited to well-established, verifiable claims.
MODELS = [
dict(slug="car-engine", name="Car engine", short="Engine", accent="#ff453a", tag="Machines",
 title="How a Car Engine Works: Interactive 3D Four-Stroke Cutaway",
 desc="See how a four-cylinder car engine works in 3D. Watch pistons, crankshaft, camshaft, valves and spark plugs fire in order, slice it open and rebuild it.",
 card="Pistons, crank and cams firing in sync.",
 lede="Four cylinders, thousands of controlled explosions a minute, all turned into smooth rotation. Spin it, slice it and watch each cylinder fire.",
 layers=[("block","Block and head"),("moving","Moving parts"),("ignition","Ignition")], slice="z",
 parts=[
  ("block","Engine block","block","The cast iron or aluminium block contains the cylinders and the passages for coolant and oil. In this cutaway the front wall is removed so you can watch the pistons move.",None),
  ("head","Cylinder head","block","The head bolts onto the top of the block and seals each cylinder. It holds the valves, the spark plugs and the camshafts, and forms the roof of each combustion chamber.",None),
  ("pistons","Pistons and connecting rods","moving","Burning fuel pushes each piston down on its power stroke. The connecting rod turns that straight-line push into rotation at the crankshaft.",None),
  ("crankshaft","Crankshaft","moving","Offset crank pins turn the up-and-down motion of the pistons into rotation. A heavy flywheel on the end smooths out the pulses between firing strokes.","In a four-stroke, four-cylinder engine one cylinder fires every half-turn of the crankshaft."),
  ("camshaft","Camshafts","moving","Egg-shaped lobes on the camshafts push the valves open at exactly the right moment. A belt or chain drives them from the crankshaft.","In a four-stroke engine the camshaft turns at exactly half the speed of the crankshaft, because each valve opens once every two revolutions."),
  ("valves","Valves","moving","Intake valves let the air-fuel mixture in and exhaust valves let burnt gas out. Springs snap them shut again once the cam lobe has passed.",None),
  ("spark-plugs","Spark plugs","ignition","Near the top of the compression stroke a high-voltage pulse jumps the plug's gap and ignites the squeezed mixture. Watch for the flash as each cylinder fires.","This engine fires in the order 1-3-4-2, the most common firing order for inline-four engines."),
 ],
 facts=[("4 strokes","Intake, compression, power and exhaust take two full turns of the crankshaft."),("1876","Nicolaus Otto built the first practical four-stroke engine, which is why it's called the Otto cycle."),("½ speed","The camshaft turns once for every two turns of the crankshaft.")],
 related=["gearbox","jet-engine","electric-pickup"],
 play=["run"],
 article=[
 ("The four-stroke cycle", ["A petrol car engine is a machine for turning chemical energy into rotation. It does this in a repeating four-step pattern called the four-stroke cycle, first made practical by Nicolaus Otto in 1876. Each cylinder runs the cycle on its own, and the cylinders are timed so that their power strokes are spread evenly around the crankshaft's rotation.",
  "On the intake stroke the piston moves down and the intake valve opens, so the falling pressure draws in air mixed with a fine mist of fuel. On the compression stroke both valves are closed and the piston rises, squeezing the mixture into a small space at the top of the cylinder. Just before the piston reaches the top, the spark plug fires. The burning mixture expands rapidly and drives the piston down on the power stroke, which is the only stroke that produces work. Finally, on the exhaust stroke, the exhaust valve opens and the rising piston pushes the burnt gas out towards the exhaust pipe."]),
 ("From explosions to rotation", ["Each piston is joined to the crankshaft by a connecting rod. The crankshaft has offset journals called crank pins, so as the pistons go up and down the shaft turns, much like your legs turning the cranks of a bicycle. In an inline-four engine, pistons one and four move together, and so do two and three, which keeps the engine well balanced. Because the cycle takes two revolutions and there are four cylinders, one cylinder fires every half-turn. A heavy flywheel stores energy between those pulses so the output feels smooth.",
  "The valves are opened by camshafts, which are driven from the crankshaft by a toothed belt or a chain. Each valve needs to open only once per cycle, so the camshafts turn at exactly half the crankshaft's speed. Springs close the valves again, and the shape of each cam lobe decides how far and for how long a valve opens."]),
 ("Why the firing order matters", ["The order in which the cylinders fire is chosen to share out the forces along the crankshaft. The model above uses 1-3-4-2, the usual order for inline-four engines. Slow it down in Run mode and you'll see the flash move between cylinders while the valves above each one open and close in step. Real engines idle at several hundred revolutions per minute, so this all happens far too quickly to see without slowing it down."])]
),
dict(slug="jet-engine", name="Jet engine", short="Jet", accent="#ff453a", tag="Machines",
 title="How a Jet Engine Works: Interactive 3D Turbofan Cutaway",
 desc="Explore a turbofan jet engine in 3D: fan, compressor, combustor, turbine and nozzle, with spinning stages. Slice it, explode it and learn how thrust is made.",
 card="Fan, compressor, combustor, turbine.",
 lede="Suck, squeeze, bang, blow. A turbofan does all four continuously, with every stage spinning on a shared idea: push air backwards to go forwards.",
 layers=[("casing","Casing"),("rotating","Rotating parts"),("hot","Hot section")], slice="z",
 parts=[
  ("fan","Fan","rotating","The big fan at the front pulls in a huge mass of air. In a high-bypass turbofan most of that air flows around the core rather than through it.","In modern airliner turbofans, the bypass air from the fan produces most of the engine's thrust."),
  ("compressor","Compressor","rotating","Rows of small spinning blades, each followed by fixed vanes, squeeze the core air to many times atmospheric pressure before it reaches the combustor.",None),
  ("combustor","Combustor","hot","Fuel is sprayed into the compressed air and burns continuously in a ring-shaped chamber. The gas leaves far hotter, and with much more energy, than it came in.",None),
  ("turbine","Turbine","rotating","The hot, fast gas spins the turbine blades, which drive the compressor and fan through the shafts. Only part of the gas's energy is taken out here; the rest leaves as thrust.","Turbine blades run in gas hotter than the melting point of their own nickel alloy, protected by internal cooling air and ceramic coatings."),
  ("shaft","Shafts","rotating","Concentric shafts link each turbine to the compressor or fan it drives. Most large turbofans have two or three shafts that spin at different speeds.",None),
  ("nozzle","Exhaust nozzle","hot","The nozzle and tail cone shape the exhaust so it leaves as a fast jet. Pushing gas backwards pushes the engine forwards, as Newton's third law describes.",None),
  ("nacelle","Nacelle","casing","The streamlined cowling around the engine guides air into the fan, keeps the outside airflow smooth and forms the bypass duct.",None),
 ],
 facts=[("Bypass","On airliner turbofans most thrust comes from fan air that never enters the core."),("1939","The Heinkel He 178, powered by Hans von Ohain's engine, made the first jet-powered flight."),("1930","Frank Whittle filed his patent for a turbojet engine in the UK.")],
 related=["car-engine","flying-roadster","gearbox"], play=[],
 article=[
 ("Suck, squeeze, bang, blow", ["A jet engine makes thrust by speeding up a stream of air and throwing it out of the back. Engineers sum up the process as suck, squeeze, bang, blow. Air is drawn in at the front, compressed, heated by burning fuel, and then expanded through a turbine and out of a nozzle. Unlike a car engine, which repeats the same steps in a cylinder, a jet engine does all four at once in different places, so the power flows continuously.",
  "The engine in this model is a turbofan, the type used on almost every modern airliner. Its defining feature is the large fan at the front. Only a small share of the air the fan moves goes into the core of the engine; the rest is pushed around the outside through the bypass duct. Moving a large mass of air fairly slowly is more efficient and quieter than moving a small mass very fast, which is why the bypass air produces most of the thrust on today's airliners."]),
 ("Inside the core", ["The core starts with the compressor, a stack of rotating blade rows separated by fixed vanes. Each stage raises the pressure a little, and together they squeeze the air to many times atmospheric pressure. The compressed air then enters the combustor, an annular chamber where fuel is sprayed in and burned continuously. The flame never goes out in normal running, and the gas leaving the combustor is extremely hot.",
  "That hot, high-pressure gas rushes through the turbine. Turbine blades work like windmill sails, taking energy from the flow to turn the shafts that drive the compressor and the fan. The blades survive temperatures above their alloy's melting point only because cool air is fed through tiny passages inside them and because they are protected by ceramic coatings. Whatever energy the turbine doesn't take is turned into a fast jet by the exhaust nozzle."]),
 ("Shafts and speeds", ["The fan, the compressor and the turbine don't all spin at the same speed. Large turbofans have two or three concentric shafts, each joining one part of the turbine to the part of the compressor or fan it drives. The fan is big, so its tips would go supersonic if it spun as fast as the small high-pressure stages, and separate shafts let each part run at its best speed. In the model, the inner high-pressure stages spin faster than the fan."])]
),
dict(slug="gearbox", name="Gearbox", short="Gearbox", accent="#ff453a", tag="Machines",
 title="How a Gearbox Works: Interactive 3D Meshing Gears",
 desc="Watch a manual gearbox's gears mesh in 3D. See input and output shafts, gear ratios, bearings and the selector fork, then slice it open and rebuild it.",
 card="Meshing gears and ratios, animated.",
 lede="Engines like to spin fast; wheels need to start slow and strong. A gearbox trades one for the other, one pair of teeth at a time.",
 layers=[("housing","Housing"),("gears","Gears"),("shafts","Shafts and selector")], slice="z",
 parts=[
  ("housing","Gearbox housing","housing","A cast aluminium or iron case holds the shafts in exact alignment and keeps the gears bathed in oil. Here it is cut away so you can see inside.",None),
  ("input-shaft","Input shaft","shafts","The input shaft is turned by the engine through the clutch. The gears fixed to it drive their partners on the other shaft.",None),
  ("drive-gears","Drive gears","gears","These gears are fixed to the input shaft. A small drive gear turning a large driven gear trades speed for torque, which is what you need for pulling away.",None),
  ("driven-gears","Driven gears","gears","Each driven gear is always in mesh with its partner but spins freely on the output shaft until it is locked to it. The gear ratio is simply the ratio of the tooth counts.","In most modern manual car gearboxes the forward gear pairs stay in mesh all the time; changing gear just locks a different gear to its shaft."),
  ("output-shaft","Output shaft","shafts","The output shaft sends the chosen speed and torque on towards the wheels. In this two-shaft layout it turns the opposite way to the input shaft.",None),
  ("bearings","Bearings","shafts","Ball or roller bearings let the shafts spin with very little friction while holding them precisely in place against the forces from the gear teeth.",None),
  ("selector","Selector fork and collar","shafts","Moving the gear lever slides a collar along the shaft with a fork. Its dog teeth lock the chosen gear to the shaft, and a synchroniser matches their speeds first so the change is smooth.",None),
 ],
 facts=[("2.5 : 1","In the gold first-gear pair, a 12-tooth gear drives a 30-tooth gear, so the output turns 2.5 times slower."),("Opposite","Two meshing external gears always turn in opposite directions."),("Constant mesh","Manual car gearboxes keep their gears meshed and lock one pair at a time.")],
 related=["car-engine","electric-pickup","3d-printer"], play=[],
 article=[
 ("Why cars need gears", ["An engine only produces useful power across a limited band of speeds, while a car's wheels need to turn anywhere from a crawl to motorway speed. A gearbox bridges that gap. By choosing which pair of gears carries the power, it changes how many times the wheels turn for each turn of the engine, and in doing so it changes the torque, or twisting force, delivered to the wheels.",
  "The rule is simple. When a small gear drives a large one, the large gear turns more slowly but with more torque, in proportion to the number of teeth. In the gold pair in this model, a 12-tooth gear drives a 30-tooth gear, a ratio of 2.5 to 1. The output shaft turns 2.5 times slower than the input, and, ignoring friction losses, with 2.5 times the torque. That's what first gear is for: getting a heavy car moving from rest."]),
 ("Always in mesh", ["It's tempting to imagine gears sliding in and out of contact when you change gear, and some very early gearboxes did work that way. Modern manual gearboxes are constant-mesh designs. Every forward gear pair is meshed all the time. The driven gears spin freely on the output shaft on bearings, so most of them are just idling round. To select a gear, the selector fork slides a collar along the shaft. Teeth on the collar, called dogs, engage with matching teeth on the side of the chosen gear and lock it to the shaft, so that pair now carries the power.",
  "Before the dogs can engage, the gear and the shaft need to be turning at the same speed. That's the job of the synchroniser, a cone clutch built into the collar that rubs the two parts to the same speed in a fraction of a second. It's why a modern gearbox changes smoothly without the double-declutching drivers once needed."]),
 ("Teeth, shafts and bearings", ["The teeth themselves are carefully shaped, usually with a curve called an involute, so that they roll against each other smoothly and the speed ratio stays constant through each tooth's contact. The shafts are held in rolling bearings in a rigid housing that keeps the gears exactly the right distance apart, and oil splashed around inside carries away heat and stops the teeth from wearing. Press play to watch the three pairs in this model turn at their three different ratios at once."])]
),
dict(slug="3d-printer", name="FDM 3D printer", short="3D printer", accent="#30d158", tag="Machines",
 title="How a 3D Printer Works: Interactive 3D FDM Printer Model",
 desc="See how an FDM 3D printer works in 3D: frame, gantry, hotend, extruder, heated bed, belts and stepper motors. Watch a print build layer by layer.",
 card="Watch it print, layer by layer.",
 lede="Melt plastic, squeeze out a hair-thin line, repeat a few hundred times. An FDM printer is a very precise hot glue gun on a robot.",
 layers=[("frame","Frame"),("motion","Motion system"),("head","Print head")], slice="z",
 parts=[
  ("frame","Frame","frame","Aluminium extrusions bolted into a rigid frame hold everything square. Any wobble in the frame shows up as ripples on the surface of the print.",None),
  ("gantry","X gantry","motion","The gantry is a pair of rails that carries the print head from side to side. In this design the whole gantry rises a fraction of a millimetre after each layer.",None),
  ("hotend","Hotend","head","A heater block melts the filament and a brass nozzle squeezes out a thin line of plastic. A finned heatsink and a fan keep the upper part cool so the plastic only melts where it should.",None),
  ("extruder","Extruder","head","A motor-driven gear grips the filament and pushes it into the hotend at a precise rate. Too little and the print is weak and gappy; too much and it blobs.",None),
  ("bed","Heated bed","motion","The print is built on a flat, heated plate that keeps the first layers warm so they stick and don't warp. In this design the bed slides forwards and backwards for the Y axis.",None),
  ("belts","Belts and pulleys","motion","Toothed belts, reinforced with fibreglass or steel cords, turn the motors' rotation into precise straight-line movement without slipping.",None),
  ("steppers","Stepper motors","motion","Stepper motors turn in small fixed steps, commonly 200 per revolution, so the controller can track each axis's position by counting steps.","Most hobby printers use NEMA 17 steppers; the name describes the 1.7-inch faceplate size, not the motor's power."),
  ("lead-screws","Lead screws","frame","Threaded rods turned by the Z motors lift the gantry. Each turn raises it by a small, fixed distance, giving fine control over layer height.",None),
 ],
 facts=[("1989","S. Scott Crump filed the patent for fused deposition modelling; Stratasys owns the FDM trademark."),("FFF","Fused filament fabrication is the generic name used by the open-source community."),("2005","The RepRap project set out to build a printer that could print many of its own parts.")],
 related=["gearbox","car-engine","house-wall-section"], play=["print"],
 article=[
 ("Building with lines of plastic", ["A fused deposition modelling, or FDM, printer builds objects by laying down thin lines of molten plastic, one layer on top of another. The technique was patented by S. Scott Crump, whose company Stratasys owns the FDM trademark; hobbyists usually call it fused filament fabrication, or FFF. Almost every desktop 3D printer works this way, and the open-source RepRap project, started in 2005, helped make the machines cheap enough for homes and schools.",
  "The plastic arrives as filament, a long strand wound on a spool. The extruder grips it with a toothed gear driven by a motor and pushes it into the hotend. Inside the hotend a heater block melts the plastic and a brass nozzle with a tiny opening squeezes it out as a thin, hot bead. The bead fuses to the layer underneath and cools in seconds. A heatsink and fan keep the upper part of the hotend cool so the filament only softens right at the nozzle, and a fan aimed at the print helps each line set before the next one arrives."]),
 ("Moving in three axes", ["To draw each layer, the printer moves the nozzle relative to the bed in X and Y, then steps up in Z for the next layer. In the design shown here the print head runs left and right along the X gantry on a toothed belt, the heated bed slides forwards and backwards for Y, and two lead screws lift the whole gantry for Z. Other printers split the axes differently, for example by moving the bed down instead, but the principle is the same.",
  "Every axis is driven by a stepper motor, which moves in small, equal steps rather than spinning freely. Because the controller knows how far one step moves each axis, it can position the nozzle precisely just by counting steps, without measuring where it is. Limit switches or sensors tell it where zero is when it starts up."]),
 ("From model to print", ["Before printing, a program called a slicer cuts the 3D model into hundreds of horizontal slices and works out a path for the nozzle on each one: outlines first, then a lightweight infill pattern inside. It saves those instructions as G-code, a simple list of moves and temperatures that the printer follows line by line. Press Print in play mode to watch the model build a twisted vase one layer at a time."])]
),
dict(slug="house-wall-section", name="House wall and roof", short="House wall", accent="#ff9f0a", tag="Buildings",
 title="How a House Wall and Roof Are Built: Interactive 3D Cutaway",
 desc="Explore a timber-frame house wall and roof in 3D: foundation, slab, studs, insulation, sheathing, cladding, rafters, tiles and drywall, layer by layer.",
 card="Every layer from footing to tiles.",
 lede="A wall looks solid, but it's a carefully ordered sandwich. Each layer has one job: carry load, stop water, hold heat or look good.",
 layers=[("structure","Structure"),("envelope","Weather and heat"),("interior","Interior")], slice="x",
 parts=[
  ("foundation","Foundation","structure","A concrete footing and foundation wall spread the weight of the house into the ground so it doesn't sink or settle unevenly. The footing is wider than the wall above it to lower the pressure on the soil.","In cold climates footings are placed below the local frost line so freezing ground can't heave them upwards."),
  ("slab","Floor slab","structure","Concrete poured over compacted fill and a damp-proof membrane forms the ground floor. Steel mesh inside it helps control cracking.","Concrete hardens through a chemical reaction with water called hydration, not simply by drying out."),
  ("studs","Wall studs","structure","Vertical timber studs, tied together by a bottom plate and top plates, carry the roof and floor loads down to the foundation. They're spaced evenly so sheet materials can be fixed to them.",None),
  ("insulation","Insulation","envelope","Mineral wool or fibreglass batts fill the cavities between the studs. They trap still air, which slows the flow of heat through the wall in winter and summer.",None),
  ("sheathing","Sheathing","envelope","Panels of OSB or plywood fixed to the outside of the studs stiffen the frame against wind. A breathable membrane over them keeps rain out while letting water vapour escape.",None),
  ("cladding","Cladding","envelope","Overlapping boards on the outside shed rain and protect the layers behind from sun and wind. Each board laps over the one below so water runs down and off.",None),
  ("rafters","Rafters","structure","Sloping timbers run from the top of the wall up to the ridge and carry the weight of the roof covering, plus snow and wind. Where they sit on the wall they're notched with a cut called a birdsmouth.",None),
  ("tiles","Roof tiles and battens","envelope","Tiles hang on horizontal battens and overlap like fish scales, so rain runs from one to the next and into the gutter.","Fired clay roof tiles have been used for thousands of years, including in ancient Greece and China."),
  ("drywall","Drywall","interior","Gypsum plasterboard fixed to the inside of the studs gives a smooth, paintable surface and hides the wiring and insulation.","Gypsum contains chemically bound water, which is released as steam in a fire and slows the spread of heat."),
 ],
 facts=[("Load path","Roof, walls and floors all hand their weight down through rafters and studs into the foundation."),("Downhill laps","Cladding, membranes and tiles overlap so gravity carries rain away."),("Below frost","Footings sit below the depth where the ground freezes, so frost can't lift them.")],
 related=["skyscraper","suspension-bridge","3d-printer"], play=[],
 article=[
 ("A wall is a sandwich", ["The walls of a timber-frame house look like one solid thing, but they're a carefully ordered stack of layers. Each layer has a single main job: carrying weight, keeping out water, holding in heat, or giving a finished surface. Slice the model and you can follow those layers from the inside of the room to the weather outside: drywall, studs with insulation between them, sheathing, a membrane and finally the cladding.",
  "The structure is the timber frame. Vertical studs stand between a bottom plate and a doubled top plate, and they carry the loads from the roof and any upper floors down to the foundation. Fixing sheathing panels of OSB or plywood across the outside turns the frame into a stiff box that resists being pushed sideways by the wind. Between the studs, insulation batts trap still air, which is a poor conductor of heat, so less heat escapes in winter and less gets in during summer."]),
 ("Keeping the water out", ["Outside the sheathing, a breathable membrane stops liquid water while letting water vapour escape, so the timber stays dry. The cladding goes over the top. Each board laps over the one below, so rain runs down the face of the wall and drips off the bottom instead of being driven in. The roof works on the same idea: tiles hang on horizontal battens and overlap downhill like fish scales, with another membrane underneath to catch anything blown past them. Water is always encouraged to flow down and out, never in."]),
 ("From roof to ground", ["The roof structure is a series of sloping rafters running from the top of the wall to the ridge. Each one carries a strip of tiles, plus snow and wind, and passes that load into the wall at a notch called a birdsmouth. From there the load travels down the studs to the bottom plate, through the concrete slab edge and into the foundation. The footing at the bottom is wider than the wall so that the weight is spread over a larger area of soil. In cold climates it's set below the frost line, the depth to which the ground freezes in winter, so that ice forming in the soil can't push it upwards. Follow that chain of parts and you've traced the load path, the most important idea in building design."])]
),
dict(slug="skyscraper", name="Skyscraper", short="Skyscraper", accent="#0a84ff", tag="Buildings",
 title="How a Skyscraper Works: Interactive 3D Structure Cutaway",
 desc="See how a skyscraper stands up in 3D: concrete core, columns, floor plates, curtain wall, elevators and foundation piles. Slice it floor by floor.",
 card="Core, columns, glass and lifts.",
 lede="Tall buildings fight two enemies: their own weight pressing down and the wind pushing sideways. Every part of a tower answers one or the other.",
 layers=[("structure","Structure"),("envelope","Curtain wall"),("systems","Elevators")], slice="y",
 parts=[
  ("core","Concrete core","structure","A reinforced-concrete tube in the middle of the tower houses the lifts, stairs and service risers. It acts as the building's spine and resists much of the sideways force from wind.",None),
  ("columns","Columns","structure","Steel or concrete columns around the edge carry the weight of every floor straight down to the foundations. Lower columns carry more floors, so they're often larger.",None),
  ("floor-plates","Floor plates","structure","Each floor is a concrete slab, often poured onto profiled steel decking, spanning between the core and the columns. The slabs also tie the columns to the core so they work together.",None),
  ("curtain-wall","Curtain wall","envelope","The glass skin hangs off the edge of each floor and carries no building weight, only its own weight and the wind pushing on it. Aluminium mullions frame each glass panel.","It's called a curtain wall because it hangs from the structure like a curtain instead of holding the building up."),
  ("elevators","Elevators","systems","Lift cars run in shafts inside the core. In a traction lift, steel ropes run over a motor-driven sheave to a counterweight that balances the car.","In 1854 Elisha Otis publicly demonstrated his safety brake by having the hoist rope cut while he stood on the raised platform."),
  ("piles","Foundation piles","structure","Long concrete piles are drilled or driven down until they reach strong soil or rock. A thick raft on top spreads the tower's weight across all of them.",None),
 ],
 facts=[("828 m","Dubai's Burj Khalifa has been the world's tallest building since it opened in 2010."),("1885","Chicago's Home Insurance Building is often called the first skyscraper, thanks to its metal frame."),("1854","Elisha Otis's safety brake made passenger lifts, and so tall buildings, practical.")],
 related=["suspension-bridge","house-wall-section","jet-engine"], play=[],
 article=[
 ("Weight and wind", ["A skyscraper has to solve two problems at once. The first is gravity: every floor, every person and every desk has to be carried down to the ground. The second is wind, which pushes on the side of a tall building with surprising force and tries to bend it like a tree. In most modern towers these jobs are shared between a stiff central core and a ring of columns around the edge.",
  "The core is a reinforced-concrete tube that runs the full height of the building. It holds the lift shafts, stairs and the risers that carry water, power and air up the tower, and because it's a closed tube it's very stiff against bending. The columns around the perimeter carry the floors' weight straight down. Lower columns support every floor above them, so they're often bigger than the ones near the top. The floor plates, concrete slabs often poured onto ribbed steel decking, span between the core and the columns and tie them together, so the whole frame acts as one."]),
 ("A skin that hangs", ["The glass you see on the outside isn't holding anything up. It's a curtain wall: a lightweight grid of aluminium mullions and glass panels hung from the edge of each floor slab. It carries only its own weight and the wind load on it, and passes both back to the structure. Separating the skin from the structure is what allows towers to be wrapped in glass, and it lets the panels be made in a factory and lifted into place."]),
 ("Lifts and foundations", ["Tall buildings only became practical with safe passenger lifts. In 1854 Elisha Otis demonstrated a safety brake that stopped a falling platform if the rope broke, and traction lifts with counterweights followed. In the model two lift cars run up and down inside the core.",
  "All of that weight has to go somewhere. Near the surface the soil is often too soft to carry a tower, so long concrete piles are driven or drilled down to firmer ground or rock. A thick concrete raft, or pile cap, on top spreads the load between them. Chicago's 1885 Home Insurance Building is often called the first skyscraper because of its load-bearing metal frame. Today Dubai's Burj Khalifa, 828 metres tall, has held the record since 2010, using the same basic ideas at enormous scale."])]
),
dict(slug="suspension-bridge", name="Suspension bridge", short="Bridge", accent="#0a84ff", tag="Buildings",
 title="How a Suspension Bridge Works: Interactive 3D Model",
 desc="See how a suspension bridge carries its load in 3D: towers, main cables, hangers, deck and anchorages. Explode it, slice it and test yourself.",
 card="Towers, cables, hangers and deck.",
 lede="The deck hangs. The cables pull. The towers push down and the anchorages hold on. A suspension bridge is a tug of war you can drive across.",
 layers=[("structure","Towers, deck and anchorages"),("cables","Cables")], slice="x",
 parts=[
  ("towers","Towers","structure","Two tall towers hold the main cables up high. The cables press down on the tower tops, so the towers work mainly in compression.",None),
  ("main-cables","Main cables","cables","Thousands of parallel steel wires are bundled and squeezed into each main cable. The cables drape between the towers and carry almost the whole weight of the deck in tension.","Each of the Golden Gate Bridge's two main cables contains 27,572 individual steel wires."),
  ("hangers","Hangers","cables","Vertical steel ropes, called hangers or suspenders, connect the deck to the main cables at regular intervals and pass the deck's weight up into them.",None),
  ("deck","Deck","structure","The roadway is built as a stiff truss or box girder so it spreads loads between the hangers and resists twisting in the wind.","The first Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapsed in 1940 after wind set its slender deck twisting; bridge decks are now tested for aerodynamic stability."),
  ("anchorages","Anchorages","structure","Massive concrete blocks at each end grip the ends of the main cables. They resist the cables' enormous pull with their weight and their grip on the ground.",None),
 ],
 facts=[("2,023 m","Türkiye's 1915 Çanakkale Bridge, opened in 2022, has the world's longest suspension span."),("27,572","Wires in each main cable of San Francisco's Golden Gate Bridge."),("1940","Tacoma Narrows collapsed in wind, changing how bridge decks are designed.")],
 related=["skyscraper","house-wall-section","car-engine"], play=[],
 article=[
 ("Hanging a road from two ropes", ["A suspension bridge carries its deck by hanging it from two huge cables. The cables drape over a pair of tall towers and are tied down at each end into massive anchorages. Vertical hangers connect the deck to the cables every few metres. It's the form used for the world's longest spans, because steel wire is extraordinarily strong when you pull on it, and a hanging cable uses that strength very efficiently.",
  "Follow the load and the design makes sense. A truck on the deck pulls down on the nearest hangers. The hangers pull down on the main cables. The cables, in tension, carry that force along their curve to the tops of the towers and on to the anchorages. At the towers, the cables press straight down, so the towers are squeezed in compression and pass the load into their foundations. At the ends, the anchorages resist the cables' pull with their sheer weight and their grip on the rock or soil."]),
 ("Cables made of wire", ["The main cables aren't single ropes. They're spun on site from thousands of parallel high-strength steel wires, pulled across the gap one loop at a time, then squeezed into a round bundle and wrapped for protection. Each of the Golden Gate Bridge's two main cables contains 27,572 wires. Because the wires are parallel, any one breaking takes only a tiny share of the strength away."]),
 ("Stiff against the wind", ["A long, light deck can behave like a sail or a wing. In 1940 the first Tacoma Narrows Bridge in Washington State twisted itself apart in a moderate wind, through an effect called aeroelastic flutter. Since then, decks have been made stiffer against twisting, using deep trusses or streamlined steel boxes, and designs are tested in wind tunnels before they're built. Today the longest suspension span in the world belongs to the 1915 Çanakkale Bridge in Türkiye, at 2,023 metres between towers, opened in 2022. Drag the explode slider to separate the towers, cables, hangers, deck and anchorages and see how each one fits into the load path."])]
),
dict(slug="cat-anatomy", name="Cat anatomy", short="Cat", accent="#bf5af2", tag="Nature",
 title="Cat Anatomy in 3D: Interactive Skeleton and Organs",
 desc="Explore a stylised cat in 3D. Toggle skin, skeleton and organs to see the skull, spine, ribcage, heart, lungs, stomach and brain. Pet it and make it walk.",
 card="Skin, skeleton and organs, in layers.",
 lede="A hunter built for stalking, springing and squeezing through gaps. Peel back the layers to see the skeleton and organs that make a cat a cat.",
 layers=[("skin","Skin"),("skeleton","Skeleton"),("organs","Organs")], slice="z",
 parts=[
  ("skin","Skin and fur","skin","The skin and coat insulate and protect the body and are packed with touch receptors. Whiskers are thick, deeply rooted hairs that sense air movement and nearby objects.",None),
  ("skull","Skull","skeleton","The skull protects the brain and holds large, forward-facing eyes that help a cat judge distance before it pounces.",None),
  ("spine","Spine and tail","skeleton","A long, very flexible backbone lets a cat arch, twist and stretch its stride when running. The tail vertebrae continue the spine and help with balance.",None),
  ("ribcage","Ribcage","skeleton","Curved ribs protect the heart and lungs and move with the breathing muscles.","Cats usually have 13 pairs of ribs."),
  ("limbs","Leg bones","skeleton","Cats walk on their toes rather than flat-footed, which effectively lengthens the leg for speed and quiet stalking.","A cat's collarbones aren't joined to the rest of the skeleton, which helps its shoulders squeeze through narrow gaps."),
  ("brain","Brain","organs","The brain processes signals from the senses and controls movement. The cerebellum at the back coordinates the precise balance behind a cat's jumps and landings.",None),
  ("heart","Heart","organs","A four-chambered heart, like ours, pumps blood through the lungs to pick up oxygen and then around the body.",None),
  ("lungs","Lungs","organs","Two spongy lungs fill most of the chest. Air reaches tiny sacs called alveoli, where oxygen passes into the blood and carbon dioxide passes out.",None),
  ("stomach","Stomach","organs","Cats are obligate carnivores: their digestive system is built for meat, and they need nutrients such as taurine that come from animal food.","Cats can't taste sweetness, because one of the genes for the sweet taste receptor doesn't work in cats."),
 ],
 facts=[("13 pairs","The usual number of ribs in a domestic cat."),("Floating","Cat collarbones aren't attached to other bones, freeing the shoulders."),("No sweet","Cats lack a working sweet-taste receptor gene.")],
 related=["house-wall-section","3d-printer","car-engine"], play=["pet"],
 article=[
 ("Built to hunt", ["Every part of a cat's body is shaped by its life as a small predator that stalks, pounces and climbs. This stylised model shows three layers you can switch on and off: the skin, the skeleton and four key organs, the brain, heart, lungs and stomach. It isn't a veterinary diagram, but the arrangement and the facts follow standard feline anatomy.",
  "Start with the skeleton. A cat's spine is long and very flexible, which lets it arch its back, twist in mid-air and stretch its stride when sprinting. The tail continues the spine with a chain of small vertebrae and helps with balance on narrow perches. Cats walk on their toes rather than on the whole foot, a posture called digitigrade, which effectively lengthens the legs and keeps footsteps quiet. Unusually, a cat's collarbones float in muscle instead of joining the shoulder to the breastbone, so its front legs can move freely and its shoulders can fit through narrow gaps. The ribcage, usually 13 pairs of ribs, protects the heart and lungs."]),
 ("Inside the chest and belly", ["The heart has four chambers, just like ours. The right side pumps blood to the lungs to collect oxygen; the left side sends it on around the body. The two lungs fill most of the chest, and air travels down branching tubes to tiny air sacs called alveoli, where gas exchange happens. Behind the diaphragm sits the stomach. Cats are obligate carnivores, which means they must eat animal tissue to get certain nutrients, such as the amino acid taurine. They also can't taste sweetness, because one of the genes needed for the sweet taste receptor doesn't work in cats."]),
 ("Senses and the brain", ["The brain sits in a rounded skull with large eye sockets. Forward-facing eyes give overlapping fields of view, which helps a cat judge distance before it leaps. The cerebellum, at the back of the brain, coordinates the fine balance behind those leaps and landings. Whiskers are deeply rooted, nerve-rich hairs that pick up tiny air movements and help a cat feel its way in the dark. And the purr? It's produced by rapid twitching of the muscles of the larynx, the voice box, as the cat breathes in and out. Tap the cat in play mode to hear it, in spirit at least."])]
),
dict(slug="electric-pickup", name="Electric pickup", short="EV pickup", accent="#30d158", tag="Vehicles",
 title="How an Electric Pickup Truck Works: Interactive 3D EV Model",
 desc="See how an electric pickup works in 3D: skateboard battery, dual motors, suspension, wheels and body. Explode it, slice it, then drive it round a test track.",
 card="Skateboard battery and dual motors. Drive it.",
 lede="No engine, no gearbox, no fuel tank. An electric pickup puts a flat battery in the floor and a motor on each axle, then gets out of the way.",
 layers=[("body","Body and bed"),("drivetrain","Battery and motors"),("chassis","Chassis")], slice="z",
 parts=[
  ("body","Body and cab","body","The cab and body panels form a stiff, crash-protected shell for the passengers. With no engine up front, the space under the bonnet can be used for storage.",None),
  ("cargo-bed","Cargo bed","body","The open bed at the back carries loads. Its floor sits above the rear drive unit and suspension.",None),
  ("battery","Battery pack","drivetrain","Lithium-ion cells are grouped into modules inside a flat, sealed pack between the axles, often called a skateboard. Its weight sits very low, which helps stability.",None),
  ("motors","Electric motors","drivetrain","A drive unit on each axle combines a motor, a reduction gear and a differential. Electric motors make strong torque from a standstill, so a single fixed gear is usually enough.","When you lift off the accelerator, the motors can work as generators and put energy back into the battery; this is regenerative braking."),
  ("wheels","Wheels and tyres","chassis","The tyres are the only contact with the road, so they handle every bit of grip for accelerating, braking and steering.",None),
  ("suspension","Suspension","chassis","Control arms, springs and dampers let each wheel follow bumps while keeping the body steady.",None),
  ("frame","Frame","chassis","Steel rails and cross-members tie the suspension, battery and body together and carry the loads from towing and hauling.",None),
 ],
 facts=[("Regen","Motors can run as generators, turning braking energy back into charge."),("Low CG","A floor-mounted battery gives a low centre of gravity."),("One gear","Most EVs use a single fixed reduction gear instead of a multi-speed gearbox.")],
 related=["flying-roadster","car-engine","gearbox"], play=["drive"],
 article=[
 ("A skateboard with a truck on top", ["Most electric vehicles, including electric pickups, are built around a skateboard: a flat, sealed battery pack that fills the floor between the axles, with the motors, suspension and wheels attached around it. The cab, body and cargo bed sit on top. This layout puts the heaviest part of the vehicle as low as possible, which lowers the centre of gravity and helps the truck feel stable in corners. This model is a generic design, not any particular brand.",
  "The battery is made of many lithium-ion cells grouped into modules, with a cooling system and electronics that monitor every cell's temperature and charge. It stores energy as direct current. An inverter converts that into the alternating current the motors need, and controls how much power flows to them depending on how far you press the accelerator."]),
 ("Motors instead of an engine", ["This pickup has one drive unit on each axle, giving all-wheel drive. Each unit contains an electric motor, a reduction gear and a differential. Electric motors produce strong torque from zero speed and run happily across a wide speed range, so most electric vehicles need only a single fixed gear rather than a multi-speed gearbox. There's no clutch, no idling and very little to service.",
  "Electric motors can also run backwards as generators. When the driver lifts off or brakes gently, the motors slow the wheels and send energy back into the battery. This regenerative braking recovers energy that a combustion vehicle would lose as heat in its brakes, and it means the friction brakes wear more slowly."]),
 ("Chassis and handling", ["The frame rails and cross-members link everything together and carry the extra loads of towing and hauling. Independent suspension at each corner uses control arms, springs and dampers so each wheel can follow the road on its own. Open play mode and choose Drive to take the truck round a test track, with keyboard keys on a computer or an on-screen joystick on a phone, and try to beat your best lap."])]
),
dict(slug="flying-roadster", name="Flying roadster", short="Flying car", accent="#0a84ff", tag="Vehicles",
 title="How a Flying Car Could Work: Interactive 3D eVTOL Concept",
 desc="Explore a concept flying roadster in 3D: ducted lift fans, rear thrusters, wings, battery and landing gear. Take off, hover and fly through rings.",
 card="Ducted fans and thrusters. Fly it.",
 lede="Take off like a drone, cruise like a plane. This concept roadster shows the ideas behind electric vertical take-off and landing aircraft.",
 layers=[("airframe","Airframe"),("propulsion","Propulsion"),("gear","Landing gear")], slice="z",
 parts=[
  ("body","Body","airframe","A streamlined composite body holds the occupants and the systems. Its smooth shape reduces drag in forward flight.",None),
  ("canopy","Canopy","airframe","A clear canopy gives the pilot a wide view, which is especially important when hovering close to the ground.",None),
  ("ducted-fans","Ducted lift fans","propulsion","Four electric fans spin inside ring-shaped ducts and push air downwards for vertical take-off and hover. The duct shields the blades and can improve thrust at low speed.",None),
  ("thrusters","Rear thrusters","propulsion","Two rear-facing electric thrusters push the roadster forwards once it's airborne. The glow here is artistic: electric thrusters move air rather than burning fuel.",None),
  ("wings","Wings and canards","airframe","Short wings and front canards make lift in forward flight, so the lift fans can be throttled back to save energy at cruise.","A wing's lift rises with the square of its airspeed, so doubling speed gives about four times the lift."),
  ("battery","Battery","propulsion","A lithium-ion pack low in the body powers every fan. How much energy a battery can store for its weight is the biggest limit on electric aircraft today.",None),
  ("landing-gear","Landing gear","gear","Three short legs with small wheels support the roadster on the ground and let it be pushed around a landing pad.",None),
 ],
 facts=[("Many fans","Distributed electric propulsion uses several smaller motors instead of one big engine."),("Lift ∝ v²","A wing's lift grows with the square of airspeed."),("Hover costs","Hovering needs far more power than cruising on wings.")],
 related=["electric-pickup","jet-engine","car-engine"], play=["fly"],
 article=[
 ("Two ways to stay up", ["This roadster is a concept, not a real product, but it's built on the same principles as the electric vertical take-off and landing aircraft, or eVTOLs, being developed today. It has two ways of staying in the air. At low speed four ducted fans blow air downwards, so it can lift off straight up and hover like a drone. Once it's moving, its short wings and front canards make lift the way an aeroplane's wings do, and the lift fans can ease off.",
  "That switch matters because hovering is expensive. To hover, the fans have to push down enough air every second to balance the aircraft's whole weight, which takes a lot of power. A wing, on the other hand, makes lift from the air flowing over it, and that lift grows with the square of airspeed: double the speed and you get roughly four times the lift. So the most efficient flight is fast, wing-borne cruise, with hovering kept to the take-off and landing."]),
 ("Electric propulsion", ["Instead of one big engine, the roadster uses several electric motors, an approach called distributed electric propulsion. Electric motors are small, light for their power and easy to control precisely, so a flight computer can vary each fan's speed many times a second to keep the aircraft level. With several motors, the loss of one can be handled by the others. Putting the fans inside ducts shields the spinning blades and can raise the thrust each fan makes at low speed.",
  "All of those motors draw on a lithium-ion battery mounted low in the body. Batteries store much less energy per kilogram than aviation fuel, which is the main reason electric aircraft today have limited range. Every design decision, from the streamlined body to the efficient wings, is about stretching that stored energy as far as possible."]),
 ("Flying it", ["Open play mode and choose Fly. Hold Up to lift off and climb, use the stick or the arrow keys to turn and accelerate, and steer through as many rings as you can before the clock runs out. The rear thrusters push you forwards and the lift fans hold your altitude when you let go."])]
),
]
